script = ExtResource("1_wqrxq")
lesson_id = "sort_selection"
title = "Selection Sort"
category = "data_structures"
icon_text = "→↓"
prerequisites = Array[String](["sort_bubble"])
visualization_scene = ExtResource("2_wjjx8")
concept_text = "Selection Sort finds the SMALLEST item in the unsorted part and swaps it to the front. Then it finds the next smallest, and so on.

The name comes from how it works: it SELECTS the minimum, then SELECTS the next minimum, etc.

Imagine sorting [29, 10, 14, 37, 13]:

Pass 1 — find minimum in ENTIRE array:
- Scan: 29, 10, 14, 37, 13
- Smallest = 10 at index 1
- Swap with index 0 → [10, 29, 14, 37, 13]
- Position 0 is now sorted forever

Pass 2 — find minimum in REST (starting at index 1):
- Scan: 29, 14, 37, 13
- Smallest = 13 at index 4
- Swap with index 1 → [10, 13, 14, 37, 29]
- Position 1 is now sorted forever

Pass 3 — find minimum in REST (starting at index 2):
- Scan: 14, 37, 29
- Smallest = 14 at index 2
- Already in place → no swap
- [10, 13, 14, 37, 29]

Pass 4 — find minimum in REST (starting at index 3):
- Scan: 37, 29
- Smallest = 29 at index 4
- Swap with index 3 → [10, 13, 14, 29, 37]
- SORTED!

Key facts:
- Always O(n²) — same speed for best, average, and worst case
- FEWER swaps than Bubble Sort (only one swap per pass, vs many)
- Memory: O(1) — sorts in place
- Easy to understand
- Good when SWAPS are expensive (like writing to flash memory)"

real_world_example = "Imagine you're picking teams for a game, and you want the shortest kid to be team captain (go first). You line up 5 kids and find the shortest — that's kid #2. You swap kid #2 with kid #1 (the current captain). Now kid #2 is the captain.

Next, you find the shortest kid in the REST of the line (kids 2-5). That's kid #4. You swap kid #4 with kid #2. Now kid #4 is in position 2.

You keep doing this. Each round, you find the shortest remaining kid and put them in the next slot. The line gets shorter (the sorted part grows) and you keep going.

Why is it called SELECTION sort? Because each pass you SELECT the next smallest item. The pattern is: SELECT minimum, SWAP it to the front, move to the next position.

Another example: organizing books on a shelf by height (shortest to tallest). You scan the whole shelf, find the shortest book, put it on the left. Then scan the remaining books, find the shortest, put it next to the first one. Repeat.

This is SLOWER than bubble sort in some ways (always O(n²) comparisons), but it does FEWER swaps. If each swap is expensive (like swapping two large files on a slow hard drive), selection sort might be faster than bubble sort in practice."

guided_example = "Let's trace Selection Sort on [29, 10, 14, 37, 13], one pass at a time.

Pass 1 — Find the smallest in the WHOLE array.

We scan: 29, 10, 14, 37, 13. The smallest is 10, at index 1. We swap it with the item at index 0 (which is 29).

After swap: [10, 29, 14, 37, 13]

The 10 is now in its FINAL position (index 0). We never touch it again.

Pass 2 — Find the smallest in the REST (positions 1 through 4).

We scan: 29, 14, 37, 13. The smallest is 13, at index 4. We swap it with the item at index 1 (which is 29).

After swap: [10, 13, 14, 37, 29]

The 13 is now in its final position. The sorted part is [10, 13].

Pass 3 — Find the smallest in the REST (positions 2 through 4).

We scan: 14, 37, 29. The smallest is 14, at index 2. It's already in place, so no swap.

After pass: [10, 13, 14, 37, 29]

Pass 4 — Find the smallest in the REST (positions 3 through 4).

We scan: 37, 29. The smallest is 29, at index 4. We swap it with the item at index 3 (which is 37).

After swap: [10, 13, 14, 29, 37]

SORTED!

Notice: each pass finds ONE new minimum and places it in its final spot. After n-1 passes, the whole array is sorted.

For 5 items: 4 passes. For each pass, we scan the remaining unsorted part. Total comparisons: about 10 (4 + 3 + 2 + 1). Total swaps: 3 (only when we actually need to move something)."

recap_text = "- Selection Sort finds the minimum in the unsorted part and swaps it to the front
- After each pass, one more value is locked in at the left
- Always O(n²) — same speed for best, average, and worst
- FEWER swaps than Bubble Sort (1 per pass vs many)
- Memory: O(1) — sorts in place
- Very simple to understand
- Good when swaps are expensive (flash memory, large records)
- Not great for large lists (always slow)
- Difference from Bubble Sort: Bubble Sort swaps many times per pass, Selection Sort swaps at most once per pass
- Used in: teaching, situations where minimizing writes/swaps matters"

